msgid "Where traffic is sent: a gateway, a line or tunnel, direct, or blocked."
msgstr ""

msgid "Another device on the LAN that forwards traffic, such as a router, computer or NAS running a proxy."
msgstr ""

msgid "An interface of this router, such as a WireGuard tunnel or a second WAN line. Traffic leaves through it with masquerading by the firewall zone it belongs to."
msgstr ""

msgid "A rule group is a set of xray-format rules (geosite, geoip, domains, IP ranges); policies match destinations by rule group."
msgstr ""

msgid "A destination matches when it belongs to any of the selected rule groups. \"All\" means any destination."
msgstr ""

msgid "Device groups select devices in policies. A device can belong to several groups and is only remembered once it is in a group."
msgstr ""

msgid "All devices on the LAN, including devices not in any group."
msgstr ""

msgid "Invert: matches when the destination is not in the selected rule groups. For example, \"Mainland China\" inverted matches destinations outside mainland China."
msgstr ""

msgid "If the gateway does not support IPv6, device IPv6 traffic needs separate handling, otherwise it bypasses the gateway."
msgstr ""

msgid "The IPv6 link-local address of the gateway, used to forward IPv6 traffic. Derived from the gateway MAC or found on the LAN."
msgstr ""

msgid "The exit is checked periodically. After two failed checks in a row, new connections use the exit selected here; the original exit is used again once it recovers."
msgstr ""

msgid "By default the gateway itself is checked. With an external address (for example 8.8.8.8) the check goes through this exit, which also detects an exit that is up but has no Internet access."
msgstr ""

msgid "By default the IPv6 next hop of the gateway is checked. If IPv6 fails while IPv4 works, IPv6 through this exit is rejected so that devices fall back to IPv4."
msgstr ""

msgid "A range covers consecutive addresses, for example 10.8.0.0/24 is 10.8.0.0 to 10.8.0.255. Use it for devices whose MAC cannot be seen, such as devices behind another router."
msgstr ""

msgid "A random MAC generated by the privacy feature of phones. Set Wi-Fi to use the device MAC or a fixed MAC per network, otherwise the device cannot be identified reliably."
msgstr ""

msgid "Manufacturer looked up by MAC prefix, to help identify unnamed devices."
msgstr ""

msgid "Devices that recently got an address from this router or were seen on the LAN."
msgstr ""

msgid "MAC rule: matches the IPv4 and IPv6 traffic of the device by MAC; IP changes do not matter. IP rule: matches IPv4 traffic by IP; IPv6 traffic is matched through the MAC that currently has this IP."
msgstr ""

msgid "IPv6 packets carry no IPv4 address. An IP rule needs the MAC that has the IP to recognise the IPv6 traffic of the device."
msgstr ""

msgid "No MAC found for this IP (the device is offline, or behind another router or VPN), so its IPv6 traffic is not steered. Use a MAC rule instead, or add the IPv6 range of the device."
msgstr ""

msgid "For devices identified by MAC, the current IP comes from DHCP leases and the neighbour table and is shown for reference only."
msgstr ""

msgid "Supports geosite:, geoip:, domain:, full:, ext: and IP ranges; domain and IP rules can be mixed in one group."
msgstr ""

msgid "The firewall option \"Drop invalid packets\". When enabled, traffic forwarded to a gateway is taken as invalid and dropped."
msgstr ""

msgid "Policies are matched in order and the first match applies; without a match, traffic is forwarded as usual by this router."
msgstr ""

msgid "keyword: and regexp: rules need the full domain to be compared and cannot be done efficiently on a router; they are skipped."
msgstr ""

msgid "full: is treated as domain: on the router, so subdomains also match."
msgstr ""

msgid "geosite.dat and geoip.dat are the common v2ray/xray rule data files; geosite: and geoip: rules are looked up in them."
msgstr ""

msgid "Plain DNS answers passing this router are recorded per device as \"domain → IP\" for domain rules. Encrypted DNS (DoH/DoT) cannot be read."
msgstr ""

msgid "Rejects DNS over TLS / QUIC (port 853) from devices so that they fall back to plain DNS and domain rules apply. Devices with a fixed private DNS hostname (for example on Android) can then no longer resolve names."
msgstr ""

msgid "Optional. Plain DNS queries from matched devices for matched domains are sent to this server through this exit, for example the gateway address or 8.8.8.8. The IPv6 DNS server is found automatically. The server must return real addresses, not fake-ip. Local names such as .lan are always resolved by this router."
msgstr ""

msgid "The DNS server is checked at the check interval by resolving the check domain through this exit. Two failures in a row mark it as failed; two successes mark it as recovered."
msgstr ""

msgid "Applies when no exit in the fallback chain can carry IPv6 DNS queries, for example when IPv6 is not handled or the IPv6 check failed. Rejecting makes devices retry over IPv4 at once, so both answers come from the same exit."
msgstr ""

msgid "A domain that this DNS server should be able to resolve. Defaults to www.google.com."
msgstr ""

msgid "Many ImmortalWrt routers redirect all DNS queries to themselves. If the gateway resolves through a plain DNS server such as 8.8.8.8, its queries would be answered by this router instead. Keep this enabled unless that redirection is wanted."
msgstr ""

